---
/**
 * /deadlines/ — the dates that catch New Bern businesses out.
 *
 * NOT A ROW IN demos.ts, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. D22
 * derives every "N live tools" count on this site from demos.length, and this is
 * not a tool: it computes nothing from the visitor's numbers and it is not
 * something anybody buys. Putting it in that array would inflate a count with
 * something that is not what the count claims to be.
 *
 * IT IS THE ONE PAGE ON THIS SITE THAT ROTS BY THE CALENDAR. Every tool computes
 * from the visitor's own inputs and so cannot be wrong about the world. This
 * makes claims about the world — a form is due on a date, a threshold is a
 * number of employees — and those go quietly false when a rule changes, with
 * nothing erroring. So every row carries its authority, a live link and the day
 * it was last read, and `npm run gate` fails on a row missing any of the three
 * or read longer ago than the age limit in the data file (D19).
 *
 * Free and ungated, like /check/. There is no form on it.
 */
import MarketingLayout from '../layouts/MarketingLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import DeadlineFinder from '../components/DeadlineFinder.jsx';
import IslandSkeleton from '../components/IslandSkeleton.astro';
import { DEADLINES } from '../data/deadlines';

/* Derived, never typed — the same rule the tool count follows. */
const sourceCount = new Set(DEADLINES.map((d) => d.authority)).size;
---
<MarketingLayout
  title="What is due, and when — New Bern, NC"
  description="A free list of the filing and permit dates that catch small businesses out in Craven County. Every one linked to whoever published it. No signup, nothing sent anywhere."
  image="/og/default.png"
>
  {/* ── The claim ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      D113 CARRIED OFF THE HOME PAGE, 2026-09-13. This page ran three bands at
      max-w-6xl, 4xl and 4xl, on one ground, with the second and third at the
      same width and the same rhythm and a hairline between them — which is the
      pair D113 exists to separate, stated exactly as /projects/ found it in its
      lab and its roadmap.

      It now walks feature, popout, popout for the column and canvas, canvas,
      deep for the ground, with the rhythm running regular, tight, tall under
      both. Every neighbor differs in at least two of those three and the
      palette does none of the work — a hue is the lightest signal on a page,
      which is the argument src/components/kit/industrial.js makes under WHY
      THREE AND NOT SEVEN.

      THE FINDER KEEPS THE PAGE'S OWN GROUND, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER
      THAN A PREFERENCE. It was built onto `deep` first, copying the shape
      /check/ settled for a page whose middle band is a working island, and
      verify-bands.mjs refused it: re-inking that band to the opposing palette
      changed not one of its eight text nodes, so the check was BLIND and said
      so. The cause is in the island rather than in the band —
      DeadlineFinder.jsx paints no color class on anything, so every word in it
      inherits `color` from <body>, resolved OUTSIDE the band, where a band's
      token re-map cannot reach it. CostCheck.jsx does carry `text-ink` and
      `text-ink-muted`, which is the whole reason /check/ can spend a ground
      here and this page cannot. Putting the finder on a painted ground would
      have been a ground nothing on this site can verify, and the next one
      somebody chose might be dark.

      SO THE HEAVIEST GROUND GOES TO THE SOURCES ARGUMENT, which is the better
      home for it anyway: that band is why the list above it can be believed at
      all, and it is the one place on the page where a word going illegible
      costs the reader something. /upkeep/, /advisory/ and /rescue/ each spend
      `deep` on the same kind of paragraph for the same reason.

      THE LAST TWO BANDS SHARE A COLUMN ON PURPOSE. The sources argument is
      addressed to the reader who has just finished the finder — moving the
      measure under them would read as a new subject rather than as the reason
      the thing above it can be trusted, which is the reading /quoted/ wrote
      down for its own coda. Ground and rhythm carry the separation instead.

      NOTHING MOVED AND NOTHING WAS CUT. The header above argues why this page
      is not a demos.ts row and why every row carries a source; both blocks sit
      where they were written. Reshaping is not reordering. */}
  <Section width="feature" class="border-b border-line">
    <Fragment slot="backdrop">
      <div class="bg-grid bg-grid-fade pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 opacity-60"></div>
    </Fragment>
      <p class="mono-label">Free · no signup · nothing sent anywhere</p>
      <h1 class="statement mt-3 max-w-3xl">
        The dates that catch people out.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Nine questions about your business, and you get back only the filing and permit dates that
        actually apply to it — each one linked to the office that published it. Nothing here is
        advice and nothing here is complete. It is a list of dates with their sources attached,
        which is more than most of us are working from.
      </p>
  </Section>

  {/* ── The finder ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Two signals against the hero — the column narrows from the widest measure
      on the page to a reading one, and the rhythm tightens. The ground is the
      signal deliberately NOT spent here; the header above says why, and it is
      the one band on this page where the reason is a failed control rather
      than a judgment.

      The tight rhythm is the same call /check/ made over its own island: the
      standard band padding stacked on something that is already tall reads as
      a gap rather than as air, which is the trap docs/page-makeover.md names
      against `density="tall"` from the other direction. */}
  <Section width="popout" density="tight">
    <DeadlineFinder client:only="react">
      <IslandSkeleton slot="fallback" name="Deadline finder" variant="chat" />
    </DeadlineFinder>
  </Section>

  {/* ── Why every line has a link on it ─────────────────────────────────────
      The page's one painted ground and its widest rhythm, against the canvas
      tight band above. The `border-t border-line` this band used to carry is
      gone because `.section-deep` paints its own hairline top and bottom as
      inset box-shadows — the class supplies the rule, so the utility was a
      second one drawn over it. */}
  <Section tone="deep" width="popout" density="tall">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink">
        Why every line has a link on it
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        A page of dates with no sources is a page you have to trust, and there is no reason to
        trust mine. Every row here names the office that publishes it and links to the page it was
        read off, with the date I last read it. {sourceCount} different authorities are cited, and
        where I could not find a primary source I have left the item off and said so rather than
        quoting a company that sells filing services.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
        Rules change and this page does not know when they do — so a row that has not been checked
        in six months fails my own build rather than sitting here looking confident. That is the
        only honest way to publish something that goes out of date on a calendar instead of on a
        code change.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8">
        <a href="/check/" class="btn btn-secondary">See what the paperwork is costing you →</a>
      </div>
  </Section>
</MarketingLayout>
